    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran’s air transport system combines airline operations, airports, air navigation and aircraft support services. Its development depends on the interaction between travel demand, available aircraft, infrastructure, regulatory permissions and international operating conditions.",
    "tags": ["Civil aviation", "Airports", "Airlines", "Transport planning", "Air connectivity"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:54:33Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:54:33Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Air Transport in Iran: Networks, Capacity and Constraints | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An evidence-based introduction to Iran’s aviation network, airport planning, fleet constraints and the limits of available traffic statistics."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "system-and-planning",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Demand and capacity must be considered together",
        "content": "Iran’s aviation system cannot be understood simply by counting airports. A paper presented by Iran to the International Civil Aviation Organization, the United Nations agency responsible for international civil aviation standards, frames network planning around travel demand, fleet availability, route costs and airport configuration. It considers aviation alongside other transport modes rather than assuming that every journey should become a flight. [s1]\n\nThe paper contrasts a baseline constrained by the existing fleet with an optimistic scenario allowing aircraft additions and modernization. These are modelling assumptions, not observed outcomes or confirmed aircraft deliveries. Their practical significance is that infrastructure and aircraft supply must be assessed together: a proposed network can require capacity that the available fleet cannot provide. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "support-services",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The services behind a flight",
        "content": "Aircraft availability is only one part of operational continuity. In a draft report for its 2025 Assembly, ICAO’s Economic Commission summarized an Iranian submission describing constraints affecting procurement, maintenance, payments, insurance and workforce stability. The submission attributed these difficulties to unilateral economic sanctions and sought international action to protect civil aviation. This is evidence of Iran’s stated position; the summary does not independently quantify the effect of each constraint or establish their relative contribution to particular disruptions. [s2]\n\nFor readers exploring this subject, that distinction matters. Maintenance, airport handling, airline management and international payments are connected operational subjects, but they require different evidence. A claim about restricted access to equipment cannot by itself establish the airworthiness of a particular aircraft, while an airline’s advertised network does not demonstrate that each service operated. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "disruption",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connectivity can change faster than infrastructure",
        "content": "Recent reporting illustrates the difference between a permanent airport and a continuously available route. In July 2025, the Associated Press reported a foreign flight arriving at Imam Khomeini airport following a suspension of international air travel, with further services expected to return gradually. That report documents a reopening episode, not uninterrupted operation afterwards. [s3]\n\nOn 18 April 2026, bne IntelliNews reported another reopening of airports and a resumption of commercial services after a wartime shutdown. Its account distinguished international schedules at Imam Khomeini from domestic traffic at Mehrabad and quoted an industry representative on the need for regulatory permits before additional flights could be scheduled. The report is a dated operational snapshot; it cannot establish the route network at the October research cutoff. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-statistics",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the available statistics establish",
        "content": "The World Bank publishes an Iran series titled “Air transport, passengers carried,” identifying ICAO statistics and staff estimates as its sources. The inspected page displayed coverage through 2023 but did not expose the annual observations needed for a reproducible dataset in this research session. A numerical trend is therefore omitted. The page’s existence should not be treated as evidence that data for the cutoff year are available. [s5]\n\nA complete account still requires verified annual observations, the indicator’s full scope and airport-level statistics with consistent reporting periods. Those additions would allow the reader to distinguish changes in airline activity from changes at individual airports, rather than treating every aviation total as interchangeable. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
